# 5. Modules

A program of a few lines can keep everything in one file. Once several
programs need the same functions, or a team shares them, the functions need
a home of their own. A **module** is that home: a named file of declarations
(functions, types, traits and bindings) that programs import.

A module is also a contract. Its public declarations, with their types, are
what every importer relies on. The rest is marked `private` and can change
without breaking anyone. The checker checks every use of a module against
that contract before the program runs: a call to a function the module does
not export, or with the wrong arguments, is a check error.

A program reaches a module's functions in one of two ways. `import m` binds
the module's name, and calls are written through it, `reading.days(412, 40)`.
`from m import f` binds the function's own name, `days(412, 40)`. The rules
are the same for every module, whether a program's own or one of the
standard library: `"Dune".upper()` calls a function of the module `string`,
which every program starts with imported.

```d2
direction: down

file: "lib/club/reading.hql\nmodule club.reading" {
  shape: page
  style.fill: "#f6efe6"
}
module: "module club.reading\npublic: days, days_for_all\nprivate: rounded_up" {
  style.fill: "#eef6ee"
}
qualified: "import club.reading\nreading.days(412, 40)" {
  shape: page
}
unqualified: "from club.reading import days\ndays(412, 40)" {
  shape: page
}

file -> module: "its place under the module root lib/\ngives its name"
module -> qualified: "binds reading"
module -> unqualified: "binds days"
```

## A module is a file under a module root

A project module is an HQL file under a **module root**. Its name is its
path under the root: `lib/club/reading.hql` is the module `club.reading`, and
`lib/club/mod.hql` would be `club`. The roots come from `.hql/config.toml`:

```toml
[modules]
roots = ["lib"]
```

or from `--modules lib` on the command line. The reading club's project
uses `lib/` as its root, and keeps the book's modules there. Roots that apply
outside any project come from `~/.hql/modules/`, from `[modules] roots` in
`~/.hql/config.toml`, and from `$HQL_PATH`. The
[[13-working-with-hql#the-execution-context|execution context]] lists them
all. A module found under two roots is an error.

Every module file starts with its name, and the checker compares the name
with the file's path under its root. This is `lib/club/reading.hql`:

````hql
/// Reading plans: how long a book takes at a number of pages a day.
module club.reading

private fn rounded_up(n: Int, d: Int) : Int = (n + d - 1) / d

/// Days to read `pages` at `per_day` pages a day; a part day counts as one.
fn days(pages: Int, per_day: Int) : Int = rounded_up(pages, per_day)

/// Days to read every book of `shelf`, one after another.
fn days_for_all(shelf: List<Int>, per_day: Int) : Int {
    each = shelf | map(pages => days(pages, per_day))
    each | sum
}
````

A `///` line is a **doc string**. It documents the declaration after it, and
`hql help club.reading` prints it. `private` keeps `rounded_up` inside the
module; [[visibility|Visibility]] covers this.

## Importing a module

`import club.reading` makes the module visible under its last segment,
`reading`, and its functions are called through that name:

```hql
import club.reading

[reading.days(412, 40), reading.days(280, 40), reading.days_for_all([412, 280, 310], 40)]
```

```text
[11, 7, 26]
```

`import a.b as c` makes it visible under `c` instead:

```hql
import club.reading as plan

plan.days(412, 40)
```

```text
11
```

A module's function is a value like any other, and can be partially
applied:

```hql
import club.reading

[412, 280] | map(reading.days(_, 40))
```

```text
[11, 7]
```

`from club.reading import days` binds the function itself, and
[[05-modules/imports|Calling a module's functions]] covers that statement:

```hql
from club.reading import days

days(412, 40)
```

```text
11
```

Imports come first in a file, before its declarations and expressions. Two
imports cannot use the same local name:

```hql
import club.reading
import club.titles as reading
1
```

```text
check error[unknown-name]: `reading` already names the module `club.reading` here; import `club.titles` under another name with `as`
 --> <expression>:2:1
  |
2 | import club.titles as reading
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
```

A module that is not imported cannot be used:

```hql
reading.days(412, 40)
```

```text
check error[unknown-name]: `reading` is not bound
 --> <expression>:1:1
  |
1 | reading.days(412, 40)
  | ^^^^^^^
```

## The standard modules

The standard library is made of modules of the same kind. The **prelude** is
the list of import statements every program starts with. It imports
`string`, `seq`, `math`, `option` and `result`, among others, both ways, so
`"a,b".split(",")`, `split("a,b", ",")` and `string.split("a,b", ",")` all
call the prelude's `string.split`. Other standard modules are imported by
name:

```hql
import regex

regex.split("a1b22c", "[0-9]+")
```

```text
["a", "b", "c"]
```

The [[reference]] has a page per standard module.

